+        <t indent="0" pn="section-4.1-3">There are three kinds of stanzas: message, presence, and IQ (short for &quot;Info/Query&quot;).  These stanza types provide three different communication primitives: a &quot;push&quot; mechanism for generalized messaging, a specialized &quot;publish-subscribe&quot; mechanism for broadcasting information about network availability, and a &quot;request-response&quot; mechanism for more structured exchanges of data (similar to <xref target="HTTP" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="HTTP"/>).  Further explanations are provided under <xref target="section-8.2.1" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="Section 8.2.1"/>, <xref target="section-8.2.2" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="Section 8.2.2"/>, and <xref target="section-8.2.3" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="Section 8.2.3"/>, respectively.</t>
